  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \n----- Overview ----- \n The upper Ohio River Valley is set to be positioned between \n an Atlantic high and western Great Lakes trough Wednesday \n through the weekend, resulting in moist airmass that allows \n for daily thunderstorm chances and varying degrees of hazard \n risks. Weak flow and forcing combined with convective evolution \n uncertainties leave lots of unknowns in  timing/coverage/hazards \n during this period.\n\nFor Wednesday/Thursday/Friday:\nEnsemble models favor the area remaining east of the upper\ntrough axis within moist, southwest flow while having the\npotential for embedded weak shortwaves to cross at varying \ntimes. Moisture currently pooled in the Carolinas will be drawn \nnorthward and push PWAT values toward the 90th percentile, \nstaying there due to little to no airmass changes (i.e. frontal \nboundaries), while enough heating creates sbCAPE values around \n1200 J/kg each day.\n\nThunderstorms are likely to be diurnally driven with total \ncoverage dependent on the presence of a mid- level shortwave \n(which could materialize based on upstream convection evolution)\nand degree of surface heating with any lingering cloud cover. \nHazardous weather threats, in the form of isolated flash \nflooding and downburst winds, thus hinge on higher lift - larger\nbuoyancy scenarios (not likely as evidence by limited outlooks \nfrom SPC/WPC) that remain difficult to discern now. Greater \nunderstanding of the environmental risks may not be fully \nrealized until the morning of.\n\nFor Saturday/Sunday:\nLatest guidance favors a more concentrated eastward progression\nof the upper trough axis this weekend, aiding greater lift to\nthe moist/unstable airmass still in place. The resultant shear\nincrease combined with similar sbCAPE and slightly higher dCAPE\nvalues seen in model mean outputs suggest a slightly higher\nsevere risk (damaging wind) when this axis crosses. This assumes\nan afternoon/evening passage that also sees enough surface\nheating to reach idealized buoyancy levels.\n\nThere is even a larger caveat to forecast outlooks during this\nperiod than the late-week section due to even larger spreads in\nshortwave timing and its progression after passage. This\nincludes ranging from quick passage Saturday with dry conditions\ndeveloping Sunday to slow passage Saturday and residual weak NW\nflow that maintains storms chances into next week. It is best\nfor users to monitor forecast trends as the weekend approaches\nand know understanding of timing and risks may remain clouded\nuntil the morning of.",
